Declare synchronized output, focus events and the OSC 52 callback

Add tsm_vte_get_sync_hold() and tsm_vte_end_sync_hold() for mode 2026,
tsm_vte_handle_focus() for mode 1004, and tsm_vte_set_clipboard_cb() for
OSC 52, with stubs: the hold is never on, no focus report is sent, and the
callback is kept but never called. A terminal can then be written against
the calls before the parser implements the modes.

--- a/src/tsm/libtsm.h
+++ b/src/tsm/libtsm.h
@@ -582,6 +582,67 @@
         unsigned int button, unsigned int event, unsigned char flags);
 
 void tsm_vte_paste(struct tsm_vte *vte, const char *data);
+
+/**
+ * @brief Whether the application holds the screen's updates.
+ *
+ * An application sets mode 2026, synchronized output, before it redraws and
+ * resets it after, so that a renderer shows no half-drawn frame. While the
+ * hold is on, a renderer keeps showing the last frame it drew. An
+ * application that dies holding the screen would freeze it, so the renderer
+ * bounds the hold and calls tsm_vte_end_sync_hold() when the bound passes.
+ *
+ * @param vte The vte object
+ *
+ * @retval true while mode 2026 is set.
+ */
+bool tsm_vte_get_sync_hold(struct tsm_vte *vte);
+
+/**
+ * @brief End the hold of mode 2026 as if the application had reset it.
+ *
+ * @param vte The vte object
+ */
+void tsm_vte_end_sync_hold(struct tsm_vte *vte);
+
+/**
+ * @brief Report that the terminal gained or lost the input focus.
+ *
+ * Sends CSI I when \c focused is true and CSI O when it is false, through the
+ * write callback, when the application has set mode 1004; sends nothing
+ * otherwise.
+ *
+ * @param vte The vte object
+ * @param focused Whether the terminal now has the focus
+ *
+ * @retval true if a report was sent.
+ */
+bool tsm_vte_handle_focus(struct tsm_vte *vte, bool focused);
+
+/**
+ * @brief The callback of OSC 52, which sets the clipboard.
+ *
+ * \c targets is the OSC's first parameter, the selections named by letters
+ * such as "c" and "p", NUL-terminated and possibly empty. \c payload and
+ * \c len are its second, the new contents in base64 as the application sent
+ * them, not NUL-terminated. Both are valid only during the call.
+ */
+typedef void (*tsm_vte_clipboard_cb) (struct tsm_vte *vte,
+				      const char *targets,
+				      const char *payload,
+				      size_t len,
+				      void *data);
+
+/**
+ * @brief Set the callback that receives OSC 52.
+ *
+ * @param vte The vte object
+ * @param clipboard_cb The callback, or NULL to ignore OSC 52
+ * @param clipboard_data The callback's last argument
+ */
+void tsm_vte_set_clipboard_cb(struct tsm_vte *vte,
+			      tsm_vte_clipboard_cb clipboard_cb,
+			      void *clipboard_data);
 /** @} */
 
